{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["And all the days of Lamekh were — (R. Shmuel son of R. Meir, of blessed memory, raised the difficulty:) Why do we need to count two wicked men who are written in the pericope? (And he resolved it:) For such is the way of the prophets, one after the other, in order to know the reckoning of years from the creation of the world until their days. For through the years and their reckoning written here, and the reckoning of the generations' years written up to Moshe Rabbenu, and the reckoning of the Egyptian servitude, as it is written: ומושב בני ישראל אשר ישבו בארץ מצרים וגו׳, and the reckoning of the forty [years they were in the wilderness], we learn the reckoning of years from the creation of the world until Israel entered the land. And the reckoning of years from the exodus from Egypt until the building of the First Temple, and from its building until its destruction, is already explicitly stated among the Prophets. And from the words of Daniel we learn the reckoning of years from the destruction of the First Temple until the destruction of the Second Temple. And he wrote concerning this: שבועים שבעים נחתך על עמך — the meaning is seventy years of the Babylonian exile, and four hundred and twenty years that the Second Temple stood — here then are seven times seventy years, and this is שבועים שבעה."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The parenthetical attribution הקשה הר\"ר שמואל בה\"ר מאיר זצ\"ל / ותירץ indicates a question raised and resolved by Rashbam himself (R. Shmuel son of R. Meir); rendered as parentheticals preserving that framing. The bracketed editorial insertions [ומנין] and [שהיו במדבר] are present in the source text and kept as brackets."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:44:22.806240109Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}